  <category>
    <pattern>DEFINE FALCONET</pattern>
    <template>A fort cf ordnance. FALDSTOOL, f.l']d-n,M. f. A kind of rtool placed at the fouth fide of the altar, at which the kings ofEng- land kneel at their coronation. To FALL, fs'l. V. n. pret. I Fell, compound pre:. I have Fai-lek or Faln. To drop from a higher place ; to drop from an erest to s prone posture ; to drop ripe from the tree; to pass at the out'et, as a river; to apostatir.e, to depart from f.iith or goodncls ; to die by vio- lence; to be degraded from an high ftation ; to enter into any ftate worse than the former ; to decrease in v.ilue, to bear less price ; to hap- pen, to besal ; to come by chance, to light on ; to come by any mif- chance to any new pcsl"eslbr; to be- come the property c-f any one by lot, chance, inheritance; to be born, to be yeaned ; To fall away, to grow lean, to revolt, to change allegi- ance ; To fall back, to fail of a pro- mise or purpose, to recedc^to give way ; To fall down, to prostrate himself in adoration, to fink, not to ftand, to bend as a fuppliant ; To fall from, to revolt, to depart from adherence; To fall in, to con- cur, to coincide, to comply, to yield to; To fall ofF, to feparate, to apostatize; To fall on, to begin eagerly to do any thing, to make an aisault; to fail over, to revolt, to delert from one fide to the other; To fall out, to quarrel, to jar, to happen, to besal ; To fall to, to begin eagerly to eat, to apply him- felf to; To fall under, to be fubjest to, to be ranged with ; To fall up- on, to attack, to attempt, to rush against. To FALL, fi'l. v. a. To drop, to let fall; to fin!:, to depress; to dimi- nish in value, to let fink in price ; to cut down, to fell ; to yean, to bring forth. F.'^LL, fa'l. f. The aisl of dropping from on high; the ast of tumbling from an erest posture; death, over- throw; ruin, distblution; downsal, loss of greatness, declension from eajinence,degradation; diminution, decrease of price ; declination or di- minution of found, ctose to musick; ^ declivity, fl:eep descent; catarast, C c 2 cascade; !s^ cascade ; the outlet of a current into any other water; Autumn, thesal! of the leaf; any thing that falls in great quantities ; the ast of felling or cutting down.</template>
  </category>
